          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-f4a49ebc495938ce\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Anatole directly avows that he loves Natasha intensely. The utterance primarily presents a loving emotional state as true. Although it advances his seduction, it does not itself undertake a commitment or obligation, and the deliberate, tender delivery supplies no evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Anatole's sincerity is doubtful and the avowal may be calculated, but deception affects contextual interpretation rather than its truth-conditional mode. Romantic pursuit alone does not establish performative commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Anatole directly addresses Natasha in quoted dialogue: «Я безумно люблю вас» ('I love you madly').\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Anatole uses the avowal as part of an aggressive, secretive courtship: Natasha has already told him that she is engaged and loves another, yet he dismisses that obstacle, arranges isolation with Hélène's help, blocks Natasha's way, moves close, and kisses her. These circumstances make the declaration plausibly strategic or manipulative, while its core force remains an avowal of love rather than a promise or spontaneous verbal reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1-core-avowal\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Я безумно люблю вас» ('I love you madly').\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration explicitly presents Anatole's intense love for Natasha as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2-prior-repetition\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Anatole says: «Я говорю, что безумно, безумно влюблен в вас» ('I tell you that I am madly, madly in love with you').\",\"supports\":\"The repeated, controlled avowal shows an ongoing courtship claim rather than words involuntarily blurted out, supporting high T and E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3-deliberate-delivery\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He takes Natasha's hand, speaks «нежным голосом» ('in a tender voice'), blocks her way, and brings his face toward hers.\",\"supports\":\"The staged and purposeful delivery supports a calculated seductive use of the avowal; tenderness and physical intensity do not independently establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4-no-undertaking\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After Natasha says she is engaged and loves another, Anatole replies, «Что мне за дело?» ('What do I care?') before reiterating his love.\",\"supports\":\"He disregards her existing commitment but makes no promise or relational undertaking of his own, supporting P=0.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T adequately captures the core avowal. Its potentially deceptive, manipulative, and coercive setting is contextual and creates no residual pressure requiring O; neither commitment force nor expressive/reflexive discharge is independently evidenced.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
